course: dictionary
lesson: 1136
title: A breeder and where animals come from
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A breeder is a person who keeps animals or plants so they can have young or grow new plants, often to sell them. When you read about puppies, kittens or rabbits, the word breeder tells you where the animal came from. «A pet should come from a rescue centre or a good breeder, and you learn how to care for the animal before choosing it.» Here a breeder is a person, and a good breeder is one who looks after the animals well.
explain: Breeder can also mean an animal or plant that has young. «Later breeders chose odd shapes too, such as bubble eyes and double tails.» Breeders here are people who picked animals with features they wanted, so the young ones kept those features. This is called selective breeding. Farmers and gardeners use it too, to grow fast horses or big tomatoes.
explain: Context helps again. «Some cheap puppies come from puppy farms where mothers are kept in poor conditions, and such puppies are more often ill or scared.» The clues cheap, poor conditions and ill tell you that not every seller is a good breeder. «A pet is best got from a rescue centre or a good breeder, after learning about it; animals sold cheaply by strangers, from vans or markets, are often ill.» So a good breeder is one you can trust, and the words around it show you what to look for.
fact: A breeder keeps animals or plants so they can have young or grow new plants.
fact: A breeder can also be an animal or plant that has young.
fact: «Good breeder» means a breeder who cares well for the animals and lets you meet the puppy with its mother.
fact: «Selective breeding» means choosing parents with the features you want, so their young have those features too.
fact: «Plant breeder» means a person who grows new kinds of plants.
exercise: «A pet should come from a rescue centre or a good breeder.» What is a breeder? | *A person who keeps animals or plants so they can have young | A cage for small animals | A person who cleans pet shops | A vet who works on animals
exercise: «Later breeders chose odd shapes too, such as bubble eyes and double tails.» What did the breeders do? | Cut the tails off every animal | *Chose parents so the young had the features they wanted | Let the animals roam wild in the hills | Sold every animal at the market in one day
exercise: «A good breeder lets you meet the puppy with its mother.» What does this tell you about a good breeder? | They only sell cats | *They are open and let you see where the puppy comes from | They never let you see the puppy | They sell puppies from vans
test: «Animals sold cheaply by strangers, from vans or markets, are often ill.» Why might a family choose a good breeder instead? | *Because animals sold cheaply by strangers are often ill | Because breeders are always cheaper | Because strangers always sell the best pets | Because vans are faster than cars
test: What can a plant breeder grow? | Bread and cakes | Only dogs and cats | *New kinds of plants | Cars and bicycles
exam: Which sentence uses breeder to mean an animal that has young? | *The old mare was a good breeder and had six foals | The plant breeder grew new roses | Ella visited the breeder with her dad | The breeder sold the puppies at the market
